•Removal is accomplished by the filing of notice of removal in federal court

•Removal requires consent of all defendants • Removal must occur within 30 days of defendants’ receipt of a pleading or other paper showing that case is removable • Removal on basis of diversity jurisdiction under Section 1332(a) cannot occur more than 1 year after filing of an action in state court, even if case is otherwise removable

NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON 28 U.S.C. § 1446
1. Removal requires the consent of all defendants. In other words, if the defendants cannot agree among themselves that they prefer federal court, then the plaintiff’s choice of state court prevails.

2. Section 1446(b) requires defendants to act promptly to remove a case once they have a basis to do so. Defendants have 30 days to file a notice of removal after receiving a complaint from which the defendants can ascertain that federal courts would have jurisdiction. For example, the complaint could allege a cause of action created by federal law, or it could seek more than $75,000 in damages in a case where the parties are diverse. And if this is not clear from the complaint but becomes clear from a later “pleading, motion, order, or other paper” (such as an interrogatory answer in discovery) that the case is removable, the defendants have 30 days from receipt of that paper to remove. Why might the removal statute require defendants to remove right away, or not at all?

3. As we already saw in Section 1441, the removal statute places special hurdles in the way of removal when the basis for original jurisdiction is diversity of citizenship under Section 1332(a). Section 1446(c)(1) forbids removal on the basis of diversity jurisdiction more than 1 year after commencement of the action in state court. In most cases, this extra limitation on removal will be irrelevant. If an action is filed in state court, and the complaint shows that the parties are diverse and the amount in controversy is greater than $75,000, then the defendants have 30 days to remove. Thus, the defendants’ chance to remove will expire long before the 1-year limit in Section 1446(c)(1). When might the 1-year limit in Section 1446(c)(1) matter?

